 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 15,
   "y": 9,
   "local": {"cy": "Goleudy Pen Strwmbl"},
   "text": "STRUMBLE HEAD LIGHTHOUSE. 1908. It flashes four times every fifteen seconds. Your teacher's laser pointer did it in assembly too."
  },
  {
   "x": 17,
   "y": 2,
   "local": {"cy": "Llwybr Arfordir Sir Benfro"},
   "text": "PEMBROKESHIRE COAST PATH. 186 miles. Please do not do it in one go."
  },
  {
   "x": 16,
   "y": 19,
   "local": {"cy": "Ynys Sgomer"},
   "text": "SKOMER ISLAND, offshore. Thousands of puffins April to July. They have gone for the winter. Not all of them. One is on that rock. Honest."
  },
  {
   "x": 12,
   "y": 21,
   "text": "LITTERED COVE. A shopping trolley, 14 flip-flops and what appears to be a full drum kit. The tide brings it, the beach keeps it."
  },
  {
   "x": 15,
   "y": 34,
   "text": "SEAL COVE. Deep water between you and the beach. Not for the faint of wade."
  },
  {
   "x": 45,
   "y": 22,
   "text": "PENTRE IFAN. A 5,500-year-old dolmen: the capstone weighs about 16 tonnes. No cranes. Stonehenge's bluestones came from these hills, too."
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 6,
   "local": {"cy": "Tyddewi"},
   "text": "ST DAVIDS 14 miles. The smallest city in Britain. Probably the one with the most gulls per resident."
  },
  {
   "x": 19,
   "y": 44,
   "text": "ST DAVIDS: follow the path. If there's a cathedral, you've gone far enough."
  },
  {
   "x": 16,
   "y": 11,
   "plaque": "pembroke_coast_last_invasion",
   "title": "The Last Invasion",
   "text": "In February 1797 a French force landed at Carregwastad Point, just east of Strumble Head, in the last invasion of Britain. They surrendered within days. Legend says Jemima Nicholas of Fishguard rounded up twelve Frenchmen with nothing but a pitchfork."
  },
  {
   "x": 16,
   "y": 21,
   "plaque": "pembroke_coast_shearwaters",
   "title": "Manx Shearwaters",
   "text": "Skomer and its neighbour Skokholm host a huge share of the world's Manx shearwaters, seabirds that winter off South America. They come ashore only at night to dodge the gulls, and their burrows wail like a ghost choir. They are named after the Isle of Man."
  }
 ],
 "spawn": [
  31,
  1
 ],
